WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.769
Question Should a sports

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:03.837
announcer is mistaken?

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:06.106
Use of the N-word cost him his job.

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:07.073
A long time

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:07.907
baseball announcer for

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.442
the Oakland A's was let go

00:00:09.442 --> 00:00:11.678
after he used the N-word on air,

00:00:12.045 --> 00:00:14.247
which he says was unintentional.

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:15.982
During a pre-game show in Kansas City,

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:16.850
Glenn Kiper,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:17.851
longtime baseball

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:18.718
announcer for the Oakland

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.220
A's, was talking to colleague

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.721
Dallas Braden

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:22.622
about their trip

00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:23.590
to the Negro Leagues

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.925
Baseball Museum, which is in that city.

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.862
But Kiper mispronounced the word Negro

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:32.365
and instead used the slur.

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:34.834
We had a phenomenal day today.

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:37.237
The League Museum

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:40.173
and Arthur Bryant's barbershop

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.775
At the time, neither Carper

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:43.610
nor Braden offered any reaction.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:44.878
Before the sixth inning,

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:48.415
Kiper told his audience this

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:50.750
little bit earlier in the show.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.886
I said something didn't come out

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:54.454
quite the way I wanted it to,

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:58.291
and I just wanted to apologize if it

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:01.528
if it sounded different than I meant it

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.265
He was then suspended by NBC

00:01:05.265 --> 00:01:07.434
Sports, California, and ultimately fired

00:01:07.434 --> 00:01:09.202
the network, said in a statement,

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:10.904
quote, following an internal review.

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:11.971
The decision has been made

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.740
for NBC Sports, California

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:14.874
to end its relationship

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:16.876
with Glenn Kiper effective immediately.

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:18.978
We thank Glenn for his dedication

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:21.281
to Bay Area baseball over the years.

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:21.714
A person

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:23.116
familiar with the investigation

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:24.451
told the Associated Press

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:25.985
that the decision was based on

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:26.252
a, quote,

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:27.387
variety of factors,

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:28.388
including information

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:30.490
uncovered in that internal review.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:31.057
We invited

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:33.193
Glenn Kiper to come on this program today

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:34.961
but he declined our offer.

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:35.895
In a written statement,

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:36.729
he called the slur

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:39.032
an unfortunate mispronunciation.

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:39.933
On that day,

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:41.668
I chose to spend my personal time

00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:43.203
by educating myself and learning

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:44.804
more about MLB's history,

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:47.273
by going to the Negro League Museum.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.842
I spent nearly 3 hours there

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:49.275
in an effort

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.310
to better understand

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:50.944
and more deeply

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:52.245
appreciate the difficulties

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:53.546
and social barriers

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:54.714
African-American players

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:56.749
endured in MLB's early years.

00:01:57.117 --> 00:01:58.551
When the subject of the museum

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:00.120
visit came up on the pregame show,

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.354
I was excited

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:03.123
and eager to share what I had done

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:05.258
and seeing that day in my excitement.

00:02:05.592 --> 00:02:07.093
I rushed through the word

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.328
Negro, resulting

00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:10.797
in my very unfortunate mispronunciation.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.365
I sincerely apologize

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:14.300
to everyone who was hurt by this.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.602
It was a terrible

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:17.070
but honest mispronunciation.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.205
I take full responsibility, he added.

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:20.940
Please know that racism is in

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.809
no way a part of me. It never has been.

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:24.177
It never will be.

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:25.311
I wish the Oakland A's

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:26.246
and NBC Sports

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:27.714
would have taken into consideration

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:28.948
my 20 year career,

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:30.116
my solid reputation,

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:31.384
integrity and character.

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:32.652
But in this current environment,

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:33.620
traits like integrity

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.855
and character are no longer considered.

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:38.158
Then the president of the museum,

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:40.493
Bob Kendrick, created a bit of a ruckus

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:42.729
when he tweeted out a statement saying,

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:44.931
My heart is one of forgiveness

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:46.666
and that he hoped others would find it

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:48.635
in themselves to do the same.

00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:50.403
I recently spoke with Bob Kendrick

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:51.504
to ask him about that

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:53.973
Mr.

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:54.541
Kendrick.

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:56.142
What struck me when I watch

00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:58.011
the tape is he said

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:00.146
he'd had a phenomenal day.

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:00.980
Obviously,

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.383
it was in praise of the museum, right?

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:07.320
It was I had met

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.990
I had met Glenn and his broadcast partner

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:13.359
Dallas Braden here at the museum

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:15.161
without a wonderful time.

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:16.362
I'm telling stories,

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:18.765
as I traditionally do, and particularly

00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:19.933
Satchel Paige, stories

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:21.201
because of Satchel's

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:24.337
connection to the AIDS franchise.

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:25.271
Of course, here

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:26.339
as a member of the Kansas

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:28.341
City Ways in 1965

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.977
when he was reportedly 59 years old,

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:32.912
and he pitched his three shutout innings

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:34.247
against the Boston Red Sox

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:34.847
giving up

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:37.083
one hit to a young Carl Yastremska

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:38.551
which was absolutely amazing.

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:39.886
So we're having a great time

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:41.554
telling all of these stories and

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:42.488
and yeah

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:42.822
let me know

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:44.023
that we're going to go eat barbecue

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:46.492
and then head over to the ballpark

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.061
and get ready for the game.

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:50.296
What do you make of critics who say

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:53.533
the word was just too accessible,

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:56.035
too much on the tip of his tongue?

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:58.438
I understand what they're saying.

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:00.940
And yes, it does

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.042
sometimes maybe make one believe

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:04.377
that is the word

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:06.412
and maybe have been used before.

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:08.281
I don't know for certain.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:10.850
So I understand that point of view.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:11.985
But again,

00:04:11.985 --> 00:04:14.120
that doesn't mean that it was deliberate.

00:04:14.554 --> 00:04:16.055
It doesn't mean that

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:17.490
in this

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:18.691
case, I felt that

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.893
there was malicious intent.

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:22.795
It was an awful thing to say.

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:25.064
We acknowledge it as such.

00:04:25.064 --> 00:04:26.566
We don't condone

00:04:26.566 --> 00:04:29.502
what the use of the word was.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:31.404
But I certainly believe

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:32.839
that was a mistake.

00:04:32.839 --> 00:04:34.173
And I've been criticized

00:04:34.173 --> 00:04:36.609
because I felt my mistake.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:38.578
I was going to ask about exactly that.

00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:39.212
What has been

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:41.414
the reaction to your forgiveness

00:04:43.816 --> 00:04:45.585
Well, no one would never think so

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:46.052
much hate

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:48.187
would come along with forgiveness.

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:50.556
Again, as I've said before,

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:52.425
this seems a little bit counterintuitive,

00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:55.561
but I understand to some degree

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:58.231
the emotional anguish

00:04:58.231 --> 00:05:00.700
that something like this can generate

00:05:01.668 --> 00:05:03.736
is a horrible

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:06.706
slur to have had happen.

00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:08.975
So I understand that.

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:11.110
Now, was I surprised by how much

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:13.112
vitriol had come my way?

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:14.380
Absolutely.

00:05:14.414 --> 00:05:15.014
But again,

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:16.783
that is the major particularly of. So

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:18.518
I hope the

00:05:18.518 --> 00:05:20.720
silver lining is a boost

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.388
in your attendance, candidly.

00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:23.323
I didn't know

00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:25.358
the museum was in Kansas City.

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:27.360
I'm not going to come back to Kansas City

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.095
without stopping in for a visit.

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:31.331
And I hope that others do likewise.

00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:31.531
Well,

00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:32.632
I hope so, too,

00:05:32.632 --> 00:05:33.399
because, Michael,

00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:35.702
we had just announced our plans

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:38.071
to build a brand new 30,000

00:05:38.071 --> 00:05:39.672
square foot standalone

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:40.406
Negro Leagues

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:41.441
Baseball Museum

00:05:41.441 --> 00:05:43.443
just a few days before this happened.

00:05:43.443 --> 00:05:46.145
So I was riding a tremendous high, man.

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:47.347
You know, this notion

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:48.514
of building a new museum,

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:49.816
even though it's going to cost us

00:05:49.816 --> 00:05:51.951
about $25 million to build it.

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:53.353
And I've gotten a lot

00:05:53.353 --> 00:05:54.420
of congratulations on

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.856
having to raise $25 million

00:05:57.123 --> 00:05:58.257
in order to do this.

00:05:58.257 --> 00:05:58.925
But I understand

00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:00.927
people are excited and we were excited.

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:02.028
And then this happens

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:03.196
and you find yourself

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:04.364
kind of embroiled

00:06:04.364 --> 00:06:05.131
in something

00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:07.033
that was really not your own doing.

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:08.935
But again, it comes with the territory.

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:09.602
And I do think

00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:10.703
a lot of people have learned

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:12.505
about the Negro Leagues Baseball Museum.

00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:14.173
And I hope we continue to see

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:14.874
a lot of people

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:16.676
who will make their way here

00:06:16.676 --> 00:06:17.844
as we record this.

00:06:17.844 --> 00:06:18.411
The Washington

00:06:18.411 --> 00:06:20.413
Nationals are walking through this museum

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:23.316
right now, and it never gets old.

00:06:23.616 --> 00:06:25.685
I always enjoy young athletes. Come here.

